   177	\section{Identity replacement and R3's measured advantage}\label{sec:reset}
   178	% BEGIN-EV2
   179	\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that the third registered budget cut (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-design}) had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}). It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. The D2 gap's market-level interval includes zero (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-results}), so we do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood. Within the D2 family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. Appendix~\ref{sec:app-results} gives the knob-level checks, the sensitivity to references that replace identities, and the search flags of this follow-up; we keep the frozen searches as primary.
