    14	\title{Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:\\Registered Tests of Age-Ranked Audits\\and an Exploratory Limit Under Honest Churn}
    15	\author{Ömer Demirel}
    16	\date{November 2026}
    17	\begin{document}
    18	\maketitle
    19	
    20	\begin{abstract}
    21	% BEGIN-EV2
    22	A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity; under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement, free replacement makes the ban costless. We study R3, a rule that audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the newest ones, in two registered simulation tests; the second was locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash. Without honest identity turnover, the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits (B3) passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six markets under the prescribed searches, and R3 and a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}). Exploratory follow-ups show that the advantage erodes when honest providers also replace their identities: R3's audits move to honest newcomers, and the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{abs_ch_fa1} to \site{abs_ch_fa5} between churn rates of $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} per round. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before they were drawn, met \site{abs_rp_met} of its \const{5} expectations with its primary search winners (\site{abs_rp_fmet} with the all-frozen winners); with the primary winners its mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.
    23	% END-EV2
    24	\end{abstract}
    25	
    26	\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
    27	A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are expensive and run after the response is served.
    28	FlashProve~\cite{rinberg2026flashprove}, for example, proves after the fact that a committed integer model produced a given token, at many times the cost of a forward pass (Section~\ref{sec:related}). If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
    29	
    30	We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks~\cite{douceur2002sybil}) are outside the model.
    31	
    32	\paragraph{Checking newcomers is an old idea.} Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host's jobs until the host passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}; customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}. Checking can also depend on what a participant reports~\cite{zarkoob2020spot}; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3's persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything else is held equal?
    33	
    34	\paragraph{The rule.} For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first $L$ rounds, auditing all of them when the round's quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/\const{2} \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits, $s$ being its served jobs (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity's first offer.
    35	
    36	\paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
    37	
    38	\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replacement identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits passes on three of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report. Within the prescribed D2-family searches, disabling the attacker's identity replacement removes most of R3's estimated gap to uniform audits; the separately searched winners can differ in every knob, so this does not isolate the value of replacement. Under honest churn, at the tested rates, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} per round and negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions, while the pooled point estimate of its advantage over uniform audits stays positive through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. Two further follow-ups continue the flagged searches and add the development runner-up comparator without churn. A prospective-exploratory replication, planned and timestamped before six new markets were drawn, meets all of its expectations with its primary search winners and all but one with the all-frozen winners: under a descriptive criterion, R3's position against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits worsens from no churn, but with the primary winners the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, the comparison with C$^*$ at the middle rate is mixed across the new markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. (iv) A record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the failed predictions, and the follow-ups' plans, released with the paper (see Availability).
    39	% BEGIN-EV2
    40	\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets and without honest churn, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC and under D2+H3 (Section~\ref{sec:results}). Q4: the registered B3--R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags. Q2 and Q3 (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-results}): the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and the registered W1 search (efficiency \site{int_w1}) supplies no efficiency bound for randomised attacks. The identity-replacement and churn follow-ups are in Sections~\ref{sec:reset} and~\ref{sec:churn}; the end of Section~\ref{sec:churn} reports the prospective-exploratory replication on new markets.
    41	Figure~\ref{fig:churn} previews the churn result of Section~\ref{sec:churn}.
    42	\begin{figure}[tb]\centering
    43	\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{figures/fig_churn.pdf}
    44	\caption{Exploratory follow-up: pooled C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 against the honest churn rate, frozen (solid) and doubled (dashed) searches. Below zero, the attacker's measured gain is higher against R3.}\label{fig:churn}
