   157	\section{The second test: results}\label{sec:results}
   158	% BEGIN-EV2
   159	The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum beside it (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-design}) adds labels and disclosures. Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest} reports the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic. Tables~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, \ref{tab:ph-doubled}, \ref{tab:ph-q4}, \ref{tab:ph-welfare}, and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens} report exploratory analyses of existing records, and Tables~\ref{tab:ph-stability} and~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} the separate registered-configuration stability check; none of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals are those of Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2}. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (\site{r_ext_n} interval entries in the report's per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit contrast there is above rounding scale on only \site{r_ext_seeds} of \site{r_ext_total} seeds (the others are at most \site{r_ext_round} in absolute value), and \site{r_ext_cnt} of the \site{r_ext_tot} frozen resamples (\site{r_ext_share}) omit all three, so their standard error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs, list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the report has a bound beyond $\const{1000}$ in absolute value.
   160	
   161	\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. As the development runner-up comparison in Appendix~\ref{sec:app-q1} shows, the registered direction holds for C$^*$, not for verified-history targeting in general. Robustness analyses in Appendix~\ref{sec:app-q1} (doubled and continued searches, market-level intervals, each rule's strongest family, and the split into attacked and reference profit) find the direction fairly stable but not the size: every upper end is near the margin, so the equivalence label is the most fragile part. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1} (its lowest bin; \site{r_hon_cs_2} in the next), and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative to C$^*$, a fraction of \site{r_hon_rel}; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available) and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}).
