   224	\section{What the results show and what they do not}\label{sec:discussion}
   225	% BEGIN-EV2
   226	\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section~\ref{sec:reset}, which holds the parameters and search budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
   227	
   228	\paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
   229	% END-EV2
   230	\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn the pooled advantage over C$^*$ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative from \const{0.03}, and the pooled advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the two lowest rates. Its prospective-exploratory replication on new markets adds the runner-up comparator but tests only two nonzero churn rates beside the no-churn baseline and a descriptive criterion, its market-level intervals mostly include zero, and its run code computed no diagnostics for continued R3 winners at $p = \const{0.03}$, so R3's audit allocation there covers only three of six markets, a gap against its plan. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
   231	
   232	\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
   233	% BEGIN-EV2
   234	For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. Under the prescribed searches and without honest churn, R3 gives the attacker a lower measured gain than uniform audits on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, and R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin. Within the prescribed D2 searches, most of R3's measured advantage over uniform audits disappears when attacker replacement is disabled. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers: the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and at $p = \const{0.05}$ the pooled point estimates of C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are \site{c_ch_d5} and \site{c_ch_q5}. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, fixed and timestamped before they were drawn, finds with its primary winners the same deterioration from no churn against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits under its descriptive criterion; at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are negative on \site{c_rp_dn} and \site{c_rp_qn} of the new markets, but at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{c_rp_d3n}, the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. The practical reading is narrow: at the tested parameters, checking the newest identities first lowers the measured gain of an attacker that is the main source of new identities, and that advantage is lost at the tested churn rates once honest turnover supplies most new identities. All follow-up intervals are exploratory and omit search uncertainty. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper (see Availability).
   235	% END-EV2
   236	
   237	\paragraph{Availability.} The repository is public at \url{https://github.com/demirelo/touchstone-registered-tests} (release v2.1, archived at \url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24498973}), and its eight record files, with their SHA-\const{256} hashes, are archived at \url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24499068}. The release identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, the identity-replacement and churn follow-ups with their run records, the prospective-exploratory replication with its timestamped plan and manifest, block-hash draw, run records, analysis, and the record digest that its paper extract is checked against, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, \texttt{docs/revision/followup2}, and \texttt{docs/revision/followup3}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
