              Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
                  Registered Tests of Age-Ranked Audits
              and an Exploratory Limit Under Honest Churn
                                        Anonymous submission

                                             November 2026


                                                 Abstract
          A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
      can return at once under a new identity; under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement,
      free replacement makes the ban costless. We study R3, a rule that audits new identities first
      and then keeps targeting the newest ones, in two registered simulation tests; the second was
      locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash. Without honest identity
      turnover, the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits (B3) passes
      on 3 of six markets under the prescribed searches, and R3 and a verified-history comparator, C∗ ,
      are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin (C∗ minus R3: 0.021). Exploratory
      follow-ups show that the advantage erodes when honest providers also replace their identities:
      R3’s audits move to honest newcomers, and the share of the attacker’s jobs it audits falls from
      0.366 to 0.042 between churn rates of p = 0.01 and 0.05 per round. A prospective-exploratory
      replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before they were drawn, met 5 of its
      5 expectations with its primary search winners (4 with the all-frozen winners); with the primary
      winners its mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and its market-level intervals mostly
      include zero.


1    Introduction
A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve [27], for example, proves after the fact
that a committed integer model produced a given token, at many times the cost of a forward pass
(Section 2). If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions
become which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
    We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are
unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before
receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the
escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population
is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged
reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks [10]) are outside the model.

Checking newcomers is an old idea. Intensive verification until an identity has earned credi-
bility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29];
BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host’s jobs until the host


                                                     1
passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results [2, 1]; customs guidance recommends checking
all declarations of new importers for a period [35]. Checking can also depend on what a participant
reports [40]; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age
and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3’s per-
sistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything
else is held equal?

The rule. For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals
whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation,
immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to
identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary 1).
R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first L rounds, auditing all of them when
the round’s quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established
identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement
is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least min(s, ⌈r/2⌉) of the r residual audits,
s being its served jobs (Lemma 2 and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a
replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer
entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity’s first offer.

Two registered tests. Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped
before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments.
The first test (October 2026) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results
then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second
test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their
seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition,
stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test’s comparison.

Contributions. (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless un-
der uniform audits (Corollary 1), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a
replacement identity (Lemma 2). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the registered
gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits passes on three of six fresh markets, all
three passes qualified by search flags; the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker
gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected
verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3’s priority
phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Exploratory follow-ups, speci-
fied after the report. Within the prescribed D2-family searches, disabling the attacker’s identity
replacement removes most of R3’s estimated gap to uniform audits; the separately searched winners
can differ in every knob, so this does not isolate the value of replacement. Under honest churn, at
the tested rates, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity
is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of
0.01 and 0.02 per round and negative on all six markets at 0.03 under both search versions, while
the pooled point estimate of its advantage over uniform audits stays positive through 0.03 and is
negative at 0.05. Two further follow-ups continue the flagged searches and add the development
runner-up comparator without churn. A prospective-exploratory replication, planned and times-
tamped before six new markets were drawn, meets all of its expectations with its primary search
winners and all but one with the all-frozen winners: under a descriptive criterion, R3’s position
against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits worsens from no churn, but with the


