-A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We show that under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the newest ones. In two registered simulation tests, the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, we compare R3 at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and a verified-history comparator, C$^*$. Without honest identity turnover, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to B3 on every fresh market (the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six), and R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); in an exploratory comparison without churn, the development runner-up, a second verified-history comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), shows no consistent direction against R3. Welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
