+A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We show that under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the newest ones. In two registered simulation tests, the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, we compare R3 at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and, in the second test, a verified-history comparator, C$^*$. Without honest identity turnover, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to B3 on every fresh market (the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six), and R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); in an exploratory comparison without churn, the development runner-up, a second verified-history comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), shows no consistent direction against R3. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$.
+Exploratory follow-ups after the registered report find that, within the prescribed searches, disabling the attacker's identity replacement removes most of R3's measured advantage over B3, and that the advantage erodes when honest providers also replace their identities. R3's audits then move to honest newcomers: with the frozen searches, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{abs_ch_fa1} to \site{abs_ch_fa5} between churn rates of $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} per round, and at $p = \const{0.03}$ and \const{0.05} both C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are lower than without churn on every sampled market. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_q5} (\site{abs_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches). These follow-ups rest on six markets, fixed parameters, illustrative churn rates, one comparator under churn, and searches of which \site{abs_ch_inc} of \site{abs_ch_cells} are flagged incomplete; their market-level intervals mostly include zero. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before they were drawn and running C-thr(\const{12}) under churn as well, met \site{abs_rp_met} of its \const{5} expectations with its primary search winners (\site{abs_rp_fmet} with the all-frozen winners); with the primary winners its mean changes rest mainly on two of the six markets, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.
