 \end{abstract}
@@ -36,11 +37,11 @@ We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identi
 \paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
 
-\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replacement identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Two exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report, that locate R3's advantage. It sits in the attacker's identity replacement: without replacement most of the gap to uniform audits disappears. Under honest churn, at the tested rates: R3 then spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} per round and negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions, while its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. A prospective-exploratory replication, planned and timestamped before six new markets were drawn, meets all of its expectations with its primary search winners and all but one with the frozen winners alone: under a descriptive criterion, R3's position against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits worsens from no churn, but with the primary winners the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, the comparison with C$^*$ at the middle rate is mixed across the new markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. (iv) A record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the failed predictions, and the follow-ups' plans, to be released with the paper.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replacement identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits passes on three of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report. Within the prescribed D2-family searches, disabling the attacker's identity replacement removes most of R3's estimated gap to uniform audits; the separately searched winners can differ in every knob, so this does not isolate the value of replacement. Under honest churn, at the tested rates, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} per round and negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions, while the pooled point estimate of its advantage over uniform audits stays positive through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. Two further follow-ups continue the flagged searches and add the development runner-up comparator without churn. A prospective-exploratory replication, planned and timestamped before six new markets were drawn, meets all of its expectations with its primary search winners and all but one with the all-frozen winners: under a descriptive criterion, R3's position against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits worsen