 \paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
 % END-EV2
-\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn the pooled advantage over C$^*$ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative from \const{0.03}, and the pooled advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the lowest rate. Its prospective-exploratory replication on new markets adds the runner-up comparator but tests only two nonzero churn rates beside the no-churn baseline and a descriptive criterion, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
+\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn the pooled advantage over C$^*$ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative from \const{0.03}, and the pooled advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the two lowest rates. Its prospective-exploratory replication on new markets adds the runner-up comparator but tests only two nonzero churn rates beside the no-churn baseline and a descriptive criterion, its market-level intervals mostly include zero, and its run code computed no diagnostics for continued R3 winners at $p = \const{0.03}$, so R3's audit allocation there covers only three of six markets, a gap against its plan. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
 
 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. Against R3 and C$^*$, the registered W1 searches selected policies with zero estimated gain and gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}; exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \co