"""Post-analysis extract for the paper (not part of the stamped manifest): R3's audit counts and full audit allocation per
winner set, rate and market, from exactly the diagnostics records the committed collector (rep_collect.collect) accepts
for expectation 5, the list of cells whose continued search produced the primary winner, the collector's status fields,
and the sha256 of the run's analysis, draw and manifest, so that replication_paper.py can refuse a stale extract.

Exactness: the collector is run unchanged. Four of its module functions are wrapped for the duration of the call only:
diag_ok and cont_diag_ok remember the diagnostics row they accept, _audit_of returns the same (audited, served) tuple
with that row's allocation and the winner's replacement switch x attached, and select_winner records which
continuation record produced which winner kind. Nothing the collector decides changes. Binding: the analysis is
recomputed from the same collected records exactly as rep_v2's analyse stage does, and the extract is written only if
it is byte-identical to the run's analysis.json.

Authentication of the extract's own fields: --check rebuilds the extract from the run records and requires it to be
byte-identical to the committed file; test_replication_extract.py pins the committed file's sha256 (EXTRACT_SHA256), so
any later edit fails the gate until the extract is rebuilt from the records and the pin is updated deliberately.

Usage: replication_extract.py --out <followup3-rep dir> --manifest <manifest> --draw <draw.json> (--write | --check) <json>"""
import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))

CATS = ("honest_replacement", "honest_initial", "focal")
STEM = re.compile(r"^(menu|cthr)-p(0|0\.03|0\.05)__fresh([0-5])@rho0\.2__(F111|Cstar|F000)__D2\+H3__profit(__churn(0\.03|0\.05))?$")
CTHR_POLICY = "Cstar"          # every cthr-directory cell is C-thr, run on the C* policy slot
WINNER_KINDS = ("continued", "unresolved")


def sha_file(p) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(Path(p).read_bytes()).hexdigest()


def records_digest(out) -> tuple:
    """(file count, sha256 over "<relative path> <sha256>" lines of every file under --out except analysis.json, sorted by
    path): identifies the run records an extract was built from, so that an archived copy can be matched."""
    out = Path(out)
    lines = sorted(f"{q.relative_to(out).as_posix()} {sha_file(q)}" for q in out.rglob("*") if q.is_file() and q != out / "analysis.json")
    return len(lines), hashlib.sha256("\n".join(lines).encode()).hexdigest()


def allocation(shares: dict) -> dict:
    """The draft-10 audit allocation (docs/revision/posthoc/churn_block.py, audit_allocation) for one winner: per-episode
    [served, audited] counts summed over episodes; young = the first age bin (younger than ten rounds); established =
    the other bins. A zero denominator gives None."""
    per = shares["per_episode"]
    nb = len(per[0]["focal"]) if per else 0
    t = {c: [[sum(e[c][b][j] for e in per) for j in (0, 1)] for b in range(nb)] for c in CATS}
    served = sum(t[c][b][0] for c in CATS for b in range(nb))
    audits = sum(t[c][b][1] for c in CATS for b in range(nb))
    yh = [t["honest_replacement"][0][j] + t["honest_initial"][0][j] for j in (0, 1)]
    eh = [sum(t[c][b][j] for c in ("honest_replacement", "honest_initial") for b in range(1, nb)) for j in (0, 1)]
    fo = [sum(t["focal"][b][j] for b in range(nb)) for j in (0, 1)]
    div = lambda a, b: a / b if b else None
    return {"young_honest_job_share": div(yh[0], served), "young_honest_audit_share": div(yh[1], audits),
            "established_honest_audit_rate": div(eh[1], eh[0]), "focal_job_share": div(fo[0], served),

\paragraph{Gains and contrasts across the churn rates.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} and Figure~\ref{fig:churn} give the pooled gains and contrasts. The gain against R3 rises from \site{ch_r3_0} without churn to \site{ch_r3_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$ ([\site{ch_r3_5_lo}, \site{ch_r3_5_hi}]), while the gains against C$^*$ and B3 change much less (from \site{ch_c_0} to \site{ch_c_5}, and from \site{ch_b_0} to \site{ch_b_5}). The C$^*$ minus R3 difference falls from \site{ch_d_0} to \site{ch_d_1}, \site{ch_d_2}, \site{ch_d_3}, and \site{ch_d_5} as $p$ rises; the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_0} without churn and \site{ch_q_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$, and then falls to \site{ch_q_2}, \site{ch_q_3}, and \site{ch_q_5}. With the frozen searches the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference changes sign between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}; with the doubled searches it is already \site{ch_d_1_d2} at \const{0.01}. The pooled B3 minus R3 gap changes sign between \const{0.03} and \const{0.05} under both search versions: at $p = \const{0.05}$ the attacker's measured gain is higher against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is more than three times the materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$, in C$^*$'s favour.

\paragraph{How firm the reversals are.} The intervals in Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} hold the six markets fixed. Treating the markets as a sample, Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-t} gives exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences for every rate and search version: every one includes zero except B3 minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the doubled searches. The churn reversals therefore have narrow seed-bootstrap intervals on these six markets, intervals that hold the selected policies fixed and omit search uncertainty, and they are not established beyond these markets. Market by market: at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on \site{ch_q_5_neg} with the frozen searches, and on \site{ch_d_5_dneg} and \site{ch_q_5_dneg} with the doubled searches (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{ch_d_5_d}; B3 minus R3: \site{ch_q_5_d}); the frozen B3 value at this rate depends on one flagged cell. At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_3_neg} of six markets with the frozen searches and on \site{ch_d_3_dneg} with the doubled ones, while B3 minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_q_3_neg} and \site{ch_q_3_dneg}, so its positive pooled value there does not hold market by market. At $p = \const{0.02}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{ch_d_2_neg} markets: one market dominates the pooled value, and with the frozen searches it sets the sign. At $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1_x} with the frozen searches and \site{ch_d_1_d} with the doubled ones, so the comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved at \const{0.01} or \const{0.02}, while the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_1_x} and \site{ch_q_1_d}. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} and \site{ch_inc_mid} of the \site{ch_inc_mid_n} at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is \site{ch_flag_max}. Reselection within each rule's frozen reference menu under churn gives a nonzero training-set reference-score change in \site{ch_resel} of the \site{ch_resel_n} cells, with a largest absolute change of about \site{ch_resel_max}; this diagnostic does not bound the change in held-out gain, and the menus themselves were not regenerated.

\paragraph{Change from no churn (exploratory, post hoc).} The intervals above concern the ordering at each churn rate. A separate question is whether churn worsens R3's relative position in the same market. Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-delta} pairs each market's contrast with its own no-churn value, per search version: at $p = \const{0.03}$ and \const{0.05}, both contrasts are lower than without churn on every sampled market under both search versions. This supports a deterioration of R3's measured relative performance on these markets, while the ordering under churn beyond them remains uncertain. These paired comparisons were computed after an external review, are unadjusted, rest on six markets, and omit search uncertainty.

\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it moves from \site{ch_fa_c1} to \site{ch_fa_c5} and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. With the doubled searches R3's value at $p = \const{0.01}$ is \site{ch_fa_1d} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}). At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. With the frozen searches, on two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (with the doubled searches, none at $p = \const{0.01}$; Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. Where the selected attack does replace (\site{ch_rep_n} markets at $p = \const{0.05}$), R3 audits between \site{ch_rep_lo} and \site{ch_rep_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_rep_lo_d} and \site{ch_rep_hi_d} with the doubled ones, and the attacker is uniquely the newest job-holding identity (by first offer, ties excluded) in only \site{ch_new_lo} to \site{ch_new_hi} of the rounds in which it holds jobs (frozen); where it stays old (the same \site{ch_stay_n} markets under both search versions), R3 audits between \site{ch_stay_lo} and \site{ch_stay_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_stay_lo_d} and \site{ch_stay_hi_d} with the doubled ones. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).

\paragraph{Efficiency.} Under its selected attack, R3's gross efficiency falls from \site{ch_eff_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_eff_5} at \const{0.05} (\site{ch_eff_5d} with the doubled searches); C$^*$'s is \site{ch_eff_c5} at \const{0.05} and changes little across the rates (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); at \const{0.05} B3's is \site{ch_eff_b5} with the frozen and \site{ch_eff_b5d} with the doubled searches, and across the rates it shows no trend in $p$; it moves between rates on single markets where the selected attack differs. On \site{ch_eff_minm} at $p = \const{0.05}$, R3's efficiency under attack is \site{ch_eff_min}, below the registered threshold of \const{0.90}; this is an exploratory point estimate, not a registered failure. The registered efficiency condition is a no-churn result.

\paragraph{Expectations.} Scored on point estimates under both search versions, all eight expectations are met: $g_{\const{0.05}}(\mathrm{R3})$ above its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e1}''); C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.05} below its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e2}''); B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at \const{0.01} (``\site{ch_e3}''); the audited share of honest replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C$^*$ and B3 at \const{0.05}, \site{ch_sh_r3} against \site{ch_sh_c} and \site{ch_sh_b} (``\site{ch_e4}''); that share under R3 falling across the four rates (``\site{ch_e5}''); C$^*$ minus R3 lower at \const{0.03} than at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e6}''); $g_{\const{0.02}}(\mathrm{R3})$ below the midpoint of the no-churn and \const{0.05} values (``\site{ch_e7}''); and B3 minus R3 positive on at least five of six markets at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e8}''). The expectations were directional and carried little risk once the timing check had been seen.
\end{table}
